            <div xml:id="co_d34">

                <pb facs="co_d34-1" n="1" xml:id="co_d34-1"/>

                <head>CO–34. Memorandum of Conversation, by the Director of the Office of West Coast
                    Affairs (Coerr)<anchor n="1" xml:id="co_d34-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1960-04-07">April 7, 1960</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Conversation Between the Secretary and President Lleras of Colombia:
                        Guerilla Problem and Survey Team.</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>PARTICIPANTS</label>
                    <item>President Alberto Lleras Camargo</item>
                    <item>Ambassador Sanz de Santamaría of Colombia</item>
                    <item>Secretary Herter</item>
                    <item>Mr. Rubottom, ARA</item>
                    <item>Ambassador McIntosh</item>
                    <item>Mr. Mann, E</item>
                    <item>Mr. Bell, U/MSC</item>
                    <item>Mr. Mau, S/S-RO</item>
                    <item>Mr. Coerr, MRA/WST</item>
                </list>
                <pb facs="0460" n="372" type="typeset" xml:id="pg372"/>
                <p>President Lleras said he had been trying to modernize the Colombian army to deal
                    with the dangerous guerilla problem (see Memorandum, same conversation, “Role of
                    the Military”),<anchor n="2" xml:id="co_d34-f2"/> and that he had thought of
                    creating two or three special battalions, but that his military officers were
                    arch-conservative in their outlook and methods.</p>
                <p>He said the United States had sent a survey mission that had, in its preliminary
                        report,<anchor n="3" xml:id="co_d34-f3"/> “recommended some conventional
                    methods that I don’t think will solve the problem”. He said he would be glad to
                    see the final report.</p>
                <p>President Lleras emphasized the Colombian military’s need for rapid transport. To
                    be effective, anti-guerilla forces must arrive at the scene of danger or
                    violence rapidly, not “two days later to pick up the dead”. He had thought of
                    helicopters. Mr. Rubottom said he understood that helio-couriers, with short
                    take-off and heavy carrying capacity, were less vulnerable than helicopters and
                    more suited to the work in Colombia, apart from being far cheaper. He said one
                    had been offered to the Colombian Government on loan, and was waiting orders at
                    Brownsville.</p>
                <p>President Lleras agreed with the preliminary report’s emphasis on the need for
                    improving the Colombian Government’s intelligence. He suggested that Colombia
                    might send up some men for training, and he was assured this could be arranged.
                    After another mention by Mr. Rubottom of the availability of the helio-courier
                    plane, the President indicated willingness to have his Government receive it for
                    trial.</p>
                <pb facs="co_d34-2" n="2" type="facsimile" xml:id="co_d34-2"/>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">[text not declassified]</hi></p>
            </div>
